  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 222 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nFrom Saturday onward, confidence in the day-to-day details drops \nconsiderably thanks to the very slow southward progression of an \napproaching cold front. While we are confident that heat relief will \narrive at some point early next week, exactly when it does so (and \nwhere) will be entirely dependent on this feature. On Saturday \nspecifically, while eroding moisture may take the edge off of our \nheat index values slightly, we have maintained our currently-valid \nHeat Advisory for south of I-70 until Saturday evening. We may need \nto make slight adjustments to the coverage for Saturday, but at the \npresent time it remains representative of expected conditions. We \nwill also need to monitor the potential for stray pulse-type showers \nagain in the afternoon, but the lowering moisture content reduces \nprecipitation chances considerably. \n\nSunday and Monday are the two most contentious days in this forecast \nperiod, as this is the most likely window for the front to move \nthrough our area. This feature will be driven south by a combination \nof convective outflows and an extension of a sprawling area of \nsurface high pressure across Ontario and Quebec. However, the upper \nflow pattern is rather unusual, with a high amplitude ridge to \nour north being undercut by weak shortwaves. While this will be \nhelpful for breaking out of the worst of the heat, because the \nfeatures at play are weakly forced and the synoptic pattern is \nrelatively nebulous, there remains quite a bit of day-to-day \nspread among ensembles. In particular temperature spreads \nincrease dramatically Sunday and Monday, and this can be \ndirectly attributed to the variance in frontal timing among \nmembers. If the front continues to trend slower (which has been \nthe case with similar fronts recently), we will need to monitor \nSunday for a possible extension of the Heat Advisory. We can't \nrule out a limited extension into Monday, even, but this is a \nmuch lower probability outcome. In any case, latest guidance has\ntrended towards a relatively sharp temperature gradient along \nthis front, with a 20-30 degree difference across the state of \nMissouri as the boundary is moving through, and at a minimum \nthis should occur area-wide by Tuesday. Behind the front, much \nmore seasonable temperatures are expected, although there remain\nrelatively high (~10 degree) temperature spreads during this \nperiod. \n\nFinally, chances for showers and thunderstorms also increase \nconsiderably as the front moves through the area, and while it is \nlikely (60-80%) that showers and thunderstorms will be observed at \ntimes between Saturday night and Monday night, the specific \"when\" \nand \"where\" will also be closely tied to the cold front timing. \nThere is also at least a modest potential for a few stronger \nthunderstorms thanks to a slight boost in wind shear, but this \npotential remains low overall. Tuesday onward, high pressure is \nexpected to shunt the deeper moisture south, and precipitation \nprobabilities drop considerably during the middle of the week.",
